---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { sunFacts, solarMassDistribution } from "../../data/solar-system";
const sunImage = "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia26/pia26681/PIA26681.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg";
---
<SiteLayout title="The Sun" description="Learn how the Sun works: nuclear fusion, its layers, magnetic activity, solar wind and the heliosphere." image={sunImage}>
  <div class="container breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/explore/">Explore</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>The Sun</span></div>
  <section class="hero-split">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">Our star · G-type main-sequence star</div><h1>The Sun</h1><p class="lede">The Sun is a G2 V star. Its gravity governs planetary orbits, and its energy drives the climates of planets with atmospheres.</p><div class="hero-chips"><span class="tag">Age ≈ 4.57 billion years</span><span class="tag">Diameter ≈ 1.39 million km</span></div><a class="btn" href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA Sun science ↗</a></div>
    <figure class="image-figure"><div class="hero-image"><img src={sunImage} alt="The Sun observed by NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory on 10 September 2025." fetchpriority="high" /><span class="image-credit">NASA / Solar Dynamics Observatory · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/image-of-sun-from-nasas-solar-dynamics-observatory/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">image record ↗</a></span></div><figcaption>Solar Dynamics Observatory image captured on 10 September 2025. The image is a scientific view of solar activity, not a visible-light photograph of the Sun.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="grid-4 sun-stats"><div class="stat"><span class="label">Whole-system mass share</span><strong>~{solarMassDistribution.nasaWholeSolarSystemEstimate.sunPercent.toFixed(2)}%</strong><small>NASA approximate inventory estimate</small></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Core temperature</span><strong>~15 million K</strong><small>Approximate central temperature</small></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Light to Earth</span><strong>~8 min 20 s</strong><small>At one astronomical unit</small></div><div class="stat"><span class="label">Power source</span><strong>Fusion</strong><small>Hydrogen nuclei become helium</small></div></div>

    <div class="callout sun-mass-method"><strong>Why you may see two different percentages</strong><p>NASA gives an approximate <strong>99.85%</strong> share for the Sun in its broad Solar System mass inventory, which includes planets, satellites and smaller bodies. Using the NASA Sun mass and JPL masses for the eight planets gives <strong>{solarMassDistribution.sunShareOfSunPlusEightPlanetsPercent.toFixed(3)}%</strong> for M☉ ÷ (M☉ + ΣMplanets). This denominator excludes moons, dwarf planets, asteroids, comets and interplanetary material. The result is a calculated ratio of the published rounded input masses, not a more precise measurement of the complete Solar System. Neither figure is an exact census of every object.</p><p>Sources: <a href={solarMassDistribution.nasaWholeSolarSystemEstimate.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA mass-distribution estimate ↗</a> · <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA Sun mass ↗</a> · <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">JPL planetary masses ↗</a></p></div>
    <div class="sun-physical-reference card">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">PHYSICAL DATA</span><h2>The Sun in numbers.</h2><p>These are rounded reference values. Mean radius describes an equivalent sphere; equatorial gravity is measured at the equator.</p></div>
      <div class="sun-physical-grid">
        <div><span>Mass</span><strong>1.9884 × 10³⁰ kg</strong><small>332,900 Earth masses</small></div>
        <div><span>Mean radius</span><strong>695,700 km</strong><small>Mean diameter 1,391,400 km</small></div>
        <div><span>Mean density</span><strong>1,408 kg/m³</strong><small>0.255 × Earth's mean density</small></div>
        <div><span>Equatorial gravity</span><strong>274.0 m/s²</strong><small>About 28 × Earth's equatorial value</small></div>
        <div><span>Escape velocity</span><strong>617.6 km/s</strong><small>From the photospheric radius</small></div>
        <div><span>Luminosity</span><strong>3.828 × 10²⁶ W</strong><small>Total radiant power</small></div>
        <div><span>Spectral class</span><strong>G2 V</strong><small>G-type main-sequence star</small></div>
        <div><span>Core temperature</span><strong>~15.7 million K</strong><small>Model-dependent central value</small></div>
      </div>
      <p class="sun-reference-note">Reference values: <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA NSSDC Sun Fact Sheet ↗</a>. The Sun rotates differentially: the equator and higher latitudes have different rotation periods.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sun-content">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>A star powered by nuclear fusion</h2>
        <p>The Sun's core is hot and dense enough for nuclear fusion. In the dominant proton–proton chain, hydrogen nuclei ultimately combine to form helium. A small difference in mass is released as energy, in accordance with Einstein's relation E = mc². That energy gradually works its way outward and eventually escapes as sunlight and other electromagnetic radiation.</p>
        <p>Gravity pulls inward while pressure generated by the hot interior pushes outward. This long-term balance is called hydrostatic equilibrium. It is not a rigid shell: the Sun is a dynamic ball of plasma, and its structure and activity change over time.</p>
        <h2>The Sun's layers</h2>
        <ul><li><strong>Core:</strong> the central region where fusion supplies the energy.</li><li><strong>Radiative zone:</strong> energy moves outward primarily through radiation and repeated absorption and re-emission.</li><li><strong>Convective zone:</strong> hot plasma rises and cooler plasma sinks, transporting energy by convection.</li><li><strong>Photosphere:</strong> the visible-light layer from which most sunlight escapes.</li><li><strong>Chromosphere and corona:</strong> the more extended outer atmosphere, visible in special observations and during total solar eclipses.</li></ul>
        <h2>Magnetic activity and the solar cycle</h2>
        <p>The Sun rotates differentially: its equator rotates faster than its higher latitudes. Together with the motion of electrically conducting plasma, this helps generate and reshape its magnetic field. Sunspots, flares, prominences and coronal mass ejections are manifestations of solar magnetic activity. The broad sunspot cycle averages about 11 years, although individual cycles vary.</p>
        <h2>The solar wind and the heliosphere</h2>
        <p>The Sun continually releases a stream of charged particles called the solar wind. This wind fills a vast region around the Sun, inflating the heliosphere. The heliosphere interacts with the surrounding interstellar medium and changes shape with solar activity and the local galactic environment.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>The heliosphere is not the edge of the Sun's gravity.</strong><p>It is a boundary shaped by the solar wind and the surrounding interstellar medium. The Sun's gravitational influence reaches vastly farther than the heliosphere.</p></div>
        <h2>How the Sun affects the planets</h2>
        <p>Sunlight drives the climate systems of planets with atmospheres, powers photosynthesis on Earth, illuminates the surfaces of airless bodies and controls much of the temperature environment. Solar wind and magnetic activity shape planetary magnetospheres, auroras and the space-weather conditions that spacecraft and astronauts must navigate.</p>
      </article>
      <aside class="card card-pad"><span class="eyebrow">A useful scale</span><h3>The Sun among other stars</h3><p>It appears unusually bright to us because it is nearby. Its light takes about eight minutes and twenty seconds to reach Earth, so we see the Sun as it was a little over eight minutes ago.</p><hr /><span class="eyebrow">Explore next</span><p><a class="text-link" href="/science/">The four fundamental interactions ↗</a></p><p><a class="text-link" href="/tools/#distance">Light-travel time calculator ↗</a></p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="source-panel"><div><span class="eyebrow">Sources</span><h3>SOURCES</h3><p>General solar structure, energy generation and solar activity summaries.</p></div><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA Science — The Sun ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/heliophysics/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Heliophysics ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/image-of-sun-from-nasas-solar-dynamics-observatory/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA Photojournal — Solar Dynamics Observatory image ↗</a></li></ul></div>
  </section>
  <style>.sun-stats{margin-bottom:44px}.sun-content{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.25fr) minmax(230px,.65fr);gap:45px;align-items:start}.sun-content aside{position:sticky;top:100px}.sun-content hr{border:0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:24px 0}.sun-content aside p{font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.75}.source-panel{margin-top:45px}@media(max-width:800px){.sun-content{grid-template-columns:1fr}.sun-content aside{position:static}}@media(max-width:600px){.sun-stats{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}</style>

  <style>
    .sun-physical-reference{padding:26px;margin:0 0 38px}
    .sun-physical-reference h2{font:500 1.8rem Georgia,serif;margin:6px 0}
    .sun-physical-reference>div:first-child p{font-size:.84rem;max-width:760px;color:#5b7083}
    .sun-physical-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:9px;margin:20px 0}
    .sun-physical-grid>div{padding:14px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:7px;background:#f5f7f8}
    .sun-physical-grid span,.sun-physical-grid small{display:block;color:#607589;font-size:.7rem}
    .sun-physical-grid strong{display:block;color:#17354e;font-size:1rem;margin:5px 0}
    .sun-reference-note{font-size:.74rem;color:#62798c;margin:0}.sun-reference-note a{color:#1b5d82}
    @media(max-width:850px){.sun-physical-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}
    @media(max-width:480px){.sun-physical-reference{padding:18px}.sun-physical-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:7px}.sun-physical-grid>div{padding:10px}.sun-physical-grid strong{font-size:.86rem}}
  </style>
</SiteLayout>
